00:22:40.770 --> 00:22:42.410
- Yeah we never said he's gonna--

00:22:42.410 --> 00:22:43.243
- How he was gonna act--
- Like Rhett,

00:22:43.243 --> 00:22:45.820
he's gonna hate Link.
- But as he entered into

00:22:45.820 --> 00:22:47.720
the dynamic of Rhett and Link,

00:22:47.720 --> 00:22:50.160
his natural sort of comedic
inclination was that

00:22:50.160 --> 00:22:52.550
wouldn't it be funny if I just hated Link

00:22:52.550 --> 00:22:55.610
and whispered weird
things to Rhett and so,

00:22:55.610 --> 00:22:58.170
there's enough of that kind
of thing that has happened

00:22:58.170 --> 00:23:00.990
over the years that there is a segment

00:23:00.990 --> 00:23:04.510
of the Mythical Beasts who are like

00:23:04.510 --> 00:23:06.400
why you gotta be picking on Link?

00:23:06.400 --> 00:23:09.800
Why is Link the butt of so many jokes?

00:23:09.800 --> 00:23:12.130
- Right.
- And so what I was asking you

00:23:12.130 --> 00:23:15.030
to do as, from a comedy standpoint--

00:23:15.030 --> 00:23:16.450
- Yes.
- Is to basically say,

00:23:16.450 --> 00:23:19.550
hey guys, that's part of our comedy

00:23:19.550 --> 00:23:21.740
and you shouldn't feel sorry for me.

00:23:21.740 --> 00:23:23.830
- Right, so--
- Right.

00:23:23.830 --> 00:23:28.830
- As a side note, I think
that the spicy pill prank

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:32.109
that was played on me was a bad example

00:23:32.109 --> 00:23:33.988
and we should come back to it.

00:23:33.988 --> 00:23:36.350
So let's not forget to come back to that

00:23:36.350 --> 00:23:39.560
'cause I think that
that's, that doesn't work.

00:23:39.560 --> 00:23:41.680
But it's a good analysis--
- Doesn't work

00:23:41.680 --> 00:23:44.360
in this context?
- It doesn't prove your point.

00:23:44.360 --> 00:23:48.406
Once we analyze it later, but
the Cotton Candy Randy thing,

00:23:48.406 --> 00:23:51.240
I mean it's obviously Jordan, of course

00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:54.430
if you don't know Jordan, somebody who's,

00:23:54.430 --> 00:23:58.530
it's not a dude, it's a fake
beard made out of cotton candy,

00:23:58.530 --> 00:24:00.235
it is a character.
- Yeah he's not a real person.

00:24:00.235 --> 00:24:04.816
- So we've entered this mode where

00:24:04.816 --> 00:24:07.620
it's not scripted, it's totally improv,

00:24:07.620 --> 00:24:11.060
but it's, we're not being
ourselves, we're just playing,

00:24:11.060 --> 00:24:16.060
we're playing things for
comedic effect entirely so,

00:24:16.300 --> 00:24:18.620
but the thing that I found interesting was

00:24:21.262 --> 00:24:22.880
even though I gave the right answer,

00:24:22.880 --> 00:24:26.450
it was a little bit different
than what you were looking for

00:24:26.450 --> 00:24:28.250
and that's fine but I
think it's fascinating

00:24:28.250 --> 00:24:30.670
so that's the only reason
I wanna talk about it.

00:24:30.670 --> 00:24:33.230
'Cause I said my answer was, well,

00:24:33.230 --> 00:24:35.300
if you ask Cotton Candy
Randy to be nice to me,

00:24:35.300 --> 00:24:39.773
I think that's a silly
request because obviously

00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:43.730
the comedy is in, and this
is me talking to them,

00:24:43.730 --> 00:24:46.490
the comedy is in him being mean to me,

00:24:46.490 --> 00:24:48.170
that's just, it's funny.

00:24:48.170 --> 00:24:53.170
And me acting as if my
feelings are hurt is part

00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:57.053
of the comedy too, like
that sells it, right?

00:24:58.170 --> 00:25:00.540
And I'm like, I wanted to
give everyone the benefit

00:25:00.540 --> 00:25:02.790
of the doubt, I didn't wanna,

00:25:02.790 --> 00:25:05.040
it doesn't get to me
as much it does to you

00:25:05.040 --> 00:25:08.948
so then at that point I didn't
tap into any frustration.

00:25:08.948 --> 00:25:11.140
I was just like, I wanna
give them the benefit

00:25:11.140 --> 00:25:14.010
of the doubt and say, now,
I don't have to tell you

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:16.270
that he's a character and
that we just made that choice

00:25:16.270 --> 00:25:18.180
but I do think there's people out there

00:25:18.180 --> 00:25:19.830
who are issuing complaints and who--

00:25:19.830 --> 00:25:23.150
- Yeah.
- Think that, but you know,

00:25:23.150 --> 00:25:25.285
then you went into this bit about,

00:25:25.285 --> 00:25:29.150
well but do you like the sympathy?

00:25:29.150 --> 00:25:31.271
And I just, I think
what was behind that is,

00:25:31.271 --> 00:25:36.271
you get more frustrated than
I do and I respect that.

00:25:37.010 --> 00:25:40.890
Because it's like, well, maybe
you should articulate it.

00:25:40.890 --> 00:25:42.440
- Well I think--
- What about it frustrates you

00:25:42.440 --> 00:25:45.210
because I think it's a valid frustration.

00:25:45.210 --> 00:25:47.780
- Well I mean, I think
there's a lot of things

00:25:47.780 --> 00:25:49.403
that fall under this umbrella.

00:25:51.668 --> 00:25:56.320
Somebody not getting the
joke always frustrates me.

00:25:56.320 --> 00:26:00.360
Somebody saying,

00:26:00.360 --> 00:26:02.736
again, this is a--
- I think when you become,

00:26:02.736 --> 00:26:05.020
when you don't get the
joke it's kinda like,

00:26:05.020 --> 00:26:07.080
oh I'm a comedian and
it didn't work but then

00:26:07.080 --> 00:26:10.350
when the byproduct is you're the bad guy.

00:26:10.350 --> 00:26:12.210
- Yeah.
- It's kinda frustrating

00:26:12.210 --> 00:26:15.000
when people, I mean...

00:26:16.850 --> 00:26:18.980
The Mythical Beasts aren't that rude

00:26:18.980 --> 00:26:21.050
but they feel like they
need to take up for me

00:26:21.050 --> 00:26:22.970
and they direct it at you.

00:26:22.970 --> 00:26:27.010
- I feel like, and again, I'm
probably generalizing, right,

00:26:27.010 --> 00:26:29.130
and I'm not, even though we know a lot

00:26:29.130 --> 00:26:31.710
of Mythical Beasts by
name or by Twitter handle

00:26:31.710 --> 00:26:34.980
and that kinda thing, no
individuals come to mind

00:26:34.980 --> 00:26:37.650
so it's not like I have
any grudges against anybody

00:26:37.650 --> 00:26:40.000
but it seems to me, in my mind,

00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:42.000
what I've constructed in my mind is that

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:48.000
there's a certain fan who,
what they like about us is just

00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:54.580
what they perceive as the
genuine Rhett and Link.

00:26:54.580 --> 00:26:58.100
But when it comes to our
comedy that we're actually,

00:26:58.100 --> 00:27:00.250
when we're trying to be comedians--

00:27:00.250 --> 00:27:02.020
- Right.
- And making comedic choices

00:27:02.020 --> 00:27:03.830
like thoughtful comedic
choices that are more

00:27:03.830 --> 00:27:06.030
of an artistic approach,
they're kinda like,

00:27:06.030 --> 00:27:06.863
I don't like that,

00:27:06.863 --> 00:27:08.880
like they don't like
our local commercials.

00:27:08.880 --> 00:27:10.630
There's a whole segment
of people, in fact,

00:27:10.630 --> 00:27:13.427
one of the reasons we stopped
making our local commercials

00:27:13.427 --> 00:27:16.170
is because we just couldn't deal

00:27:16.170 --> 00:27:17.931
with so many people misunderstanding

00:27:17.931 --> 00:27:20.330
what we were trying to do comedically.

00:27:20.330 --> 00:27:23.780
Interestingly, when we hang out with

00:27:26.610 --> 00:27:29.713
legitimate comedians,
professional comedians,

00:27:30.780 --> 00:27:33.700
a lot of times the only point
of reference they have from us

00:27:33.700 --> 00:27:36.190
is our local commercials
and so in our minds,

00:27:36.190 --> 00:27:38.470
from a comedic standpoint
is those local commercials

00:27:38.470 --> 00:27:39.980
that we made--
- That's a craft.

00:27:39.980 --> 00:27:42.887
- It's the, potentially the
best work we've ever done

00:27:42.887 --> 00:27:45.840
but there's a not an
insignificant percentage

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:47.730
of Mythical Beasts who are like,

00:27:47.730 --> 00:27:50.780
that's the stupidest thing
that you guys have ever done.

00:27:50.780 --> 00:27:54.680
And so that, I can't
come up with an analogy

00:27:54.680 --> 00:27:56.961
but it's almost just like,
if you consider yourself

00:27:56.961 --> 00:27:59.260
to be an artist and you're like,

00:27:59.260 --> 00:28:02.860
my best work is this
thing and then this thing

00:28:02.860 --> 00:28:05.230
that I kinda do incidentally,

00:28:05.230 --> 00:28:07.080
that's the thing that
you're all connecting with

00:28:07.080 --> 00:28:08.380
and you're not connecting with this thing

00:28:08.380 --> 00:28:10.350
that actually was
really, really thoughtful

00:28:10.350 --> 00:28:12.110
and is my best effort.

00:28:12.110 --> 00:28:14.840
And then I think it
carries over to, again,

00:28:14.840 --> 00:28:18.533
I think it's taking us
so seriously as people

00:28:18.533 --> 00:28:22.520
that it's just like well
sometimes the bit between us

00:28:22.520 --> 00:28:24.480
as a duo is that

00:28:25.460 --> 00:28:30.150
my character is going to make fun of you

00:28:30.150 --> 00:28:33.180
and say mean things,

00:28:33.180 --> 00:28:35.020
or some other character in our universe

00:28:35.020 --> 00:28:36.640
like Cotton Candy Randy's
gonna say mean things

00:28:36.640 --> 00:28:40.270
and then I get tweets
and I see comments about,

00:28:40.270 --> 00:28:43.590
why's he gotta be so mean
to Link and I'm like, guys.

00:28:43.590 --> 00:28:45.740
It's a freaking joke.
- And I think here

00:28:45.740 --> 00:28:47.320
on Ear Biscuits--
- And that just frustrates me.

00:28:47.320 --> 00:28:48.153
- Yeah.

00:28:50.270 --> 00:28:54.090
I think you can anticipate
that Ear Biscuits

00:28:54.090 --> 00:28:56.030
is a much more real version of who we are

00:28:56.030 --> 00:28:58.550
so I'm not holding anything
that you don't already know

00:28:58.550 --> 00:29:00.830
so in this environment,

00:29:00.830 --> 00:29:04.770
I think that I definitely make
fun of you a lot more here

00:29:05.940 --> 00:29:09.500
and the real you than I do on the show.

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:14.500
Because, but we both know that
there's comedy in that here

00:29:14.680 --> 00:29:16.790
'cause we're not just totally talking,

00:29:16.790 --> 00:29:19.760
we're making fun of each
other because it's funny.

00:29:19.760 --> 00:29:22.053
But in the context of GMM,

00:29:23.100 --> 00:29:25.240
so much of the strength of the show rides

00:29:25.240 --> 00:29:28.280
on the impromptu honestly of the moments,

00:29:28.280 --> 00:29:29.740
like there's so many
things that happen that

00:29:29.740 --> 00:29:30.760
by design we don't know.

00:29:30.760 --> 00:29:32.520
I think we've talked about this before.

00:29:32.520 --> 00:29:37.220
But there are times when
we go into comedy bits

00:29:37.220 --> 00:29:39.510
and there's...

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:43.643
That balance shifts from moment to moment.

00:29:46.160 --> 00:29:48.489
There are entire episodes that are planned

00:29:48.489 --> 00:29:51.950
like when you go into a conspiracy thing

00:29:51.950 --> 00:29:54.250
about how Finland doesn't exist.

00:29:54.250 --> 00:29:58.260
Okay, bombshell, can you
please come clean about

00:29:58.260 --> 00:29:59.450
what you think about Finland?

00:29:59.450 --> 00:30:01.550
- You mean the country that doesn't exist?

00:30:03.670 --> 00:30:05.510
- He has a choice to make here.

00:30:05.510 --> 00:30:06.790
Do you really believe--

00:30:06.790 --> 00:30:07.820
- Yeah and--
- Can you say it?

00:30:07.820 --> 00:30:10.260
Of course.
- I think that most people

00:30:10.260 --> 00:30:13.920
probably, I would say that
90, there's definitely 1%

00:30:13.920 --> 00:30:15.650
of the people watching who think

00:30:15.650 --> 00:30:18.380
that I legitimately believe
the conspiracy theories

00:30:18.380 --> 00:30:19.787
and there's nothing I'm ever--
- Just say it.

00:30:19.787 --> 00:30:21.293
You don't believe what?

00:30:23.170 --> 00:30:24.950
- I believe that Finland
is a real country.

00:30:24.950 --> 00:30:25.830
- Oh, he did it.

00:30:25.830 --> 00:30:27.560
That was a big milestone.

00:30:27.560 --> 00:30:30.100
No it's not because, and you kinda know,

00:30:30.100 --> 00:30:31.480
you've suspended your disbelief

00:30:31.480 --> 00:30:33.850
and we're going into this
bit and we like to do it.

00:30:33.850 --> 00:30:35.620
Does it get the most views?

00:30:35.620 --> 00:30:39.090
No but it's a comedic choice, you know--

00:30:39.090 --> 00:30:40.880
- And I'm actually making--
- It's more craft work.

00:30:40.880 --> 00:30:42.107
- And I'm actually--

00:30:43.970 --> 00:30:47.780
- Like the band.
- I'm actually very unlikely

00:30:47.780 --> 00:30:49.940
to believe conspiracy
theories in real life.

00:30:49.940 --> 00:30:52.060
- Yeah you're making fun, yeah.

00:30:52.060 --> 00:30:53.391
- I'm an extreme--
- You probably should have

00:30:53.391 --> 00:30:56.730
changed your name and--
- I'm an extreme skeptic right

00:30:56.730 --> 00:31:01.430
and so I actually find

00:31:01.430 --> 00:31:04.640
conspiracy theorists to be funny.

00:31:04.640 --> 00:31:06.780
Like I find them funny.
- Well I mean in terms of--

00:31:06.780 --> 00:31:08.627
- And so I think that they're--

00:31:08.627 --> 00:31:12.070
- But in terms of a mode you
look at The Colbert Report.

00:31:12.070 --> 00:31:14.990
Not the show now but the
transition is fascinating

00:31:14.990 --> 00:31:17.750
because that was brilliant.

00:31:17.750 --> 00:31:19.910
I mean there's a reason
why I think it won Emmys

00:31:19.910 --> 00:31:21.950
or should have, and he did

00:31:21.950 --> 00:31:25.370
because it was absolutely
brilliant what he did.

00:31:25.370 --> 00:31:26.458
- Right, but--
- And it worked on

00:31:26.458 --> 00:31:28.119
a number of levels.

00:31:28.119 --> 00:31:29.413
So we aspire to things like that.

00:31:29.413 --> 00:31:32.950
- What is the Venn diagram
of the people who don't like

00:31:32.950 --> 00:31:36.753
Cotton Candy Randy who also
don't like Stephen Colbert?

00:31:37.690 --> 00:31:40.200
I would say there is
a very high crossover.

00:31:40.200 --> 00:31:41.540
That--
- I know so I think

00:31:41.540 --> 00:31:44.330
what you're getting at is I
think the frustration is that

00:31:44.330 --> 00:31:49.330
there are people in our
GMM audience who they,

00:31:50.230 --> 00:31:53.700
and I think I understand
why they're like that,

00:31:53.700 --> 00:31:55.340
they wanna take everything at face value

00:31:55.340 --> 00:31:57.828
because we present it that way.

00:31:57.828 --> 00:32:01.480
I think that, I think
the reason why I wanted

00:32:01.480 --> 00:32:03.430
to have this conversation is if some

00:32:03.430 --> 00:32:07.380
of those people are listening or watching,

00:32:07.380 --> 00:32:10.430
that we can kind of educate
where we're coming from

00:32:10.430 --> 00:32:14.060
to free them up to enjoy
more of the entire breadth

00:32:14.060 --> 00:32:17.670
of what GMM is and also to kinda alleviate

00:32:17.670 --> 00:32:18.730
your frustration a little bit.

00:32:18.730 --> 00:32:22.183
I think that's kinda my
ulterior motive in this thing.

00:32:24.350 --> 00:32:27.310
There's also this complaint
about the show being scripted

00:32:27.310 --> 00:32:29.010
which again is a different thing.

00:32:29.010 --> 00:32:31.310
But I think it's related a little bit.

00:32:31.310 --> 00:32:36.033
And again, we go out in and
out of scripted moments.

00:32:37.240 --> 00:32:40.220
We shouldn't have to, I
think people are so attached

00:32:40.220 --> 00:32:42.970
to the impromptu honesty of the show that

00:32:42.970 --> 00:32:46.530
if there's anything that's
planned, like I can tell

00:32:46.530 --> 00:32:48.540
that they had thought about
that joke ahead of time,

00:32:48.540 --> 00:32:52.200
like well, we could be better
at delivering some jokes

00:32:52.200 --> 00:32:54.400
sometimes but, and the top of the show,

00:32:54.400 --> 00:32:56.259
we plan out what we're
gonna say so that we can get

00:32:56.259 --> 00:32:58.853
into it quicker so we can
just be more succinct.

00:33:00.270 --> 00:33:02.770
They're just different modes in the show

00:33:02.770 --> 00:33:07.245
that I think, I think it's an opportunity

00:33:07.245 --> 00:33:09.280
to make it more fun to say, okay,

00:33:09.280 --> 00:33:13.850
I don't know exactly,
it's all about sensing

00:33:13.850 --> 00:33:15.600
what mode we're working in.

00:33:15.600 --> 00:33:20.480
And then saying, sometimes
it's blurry and it kinda shifts

00:33:20.480 --> 00:33:23.720
and I think that's a
fun aspect of the show

00:33:23.720 --> 00:33:28.110
so I get frustrated when people criticize

00:33:28.110 --> 00:33:31.678
when we're not just acting
in the 100% impromptu mode,

00:33:31.678 --> 00:33:35.587
you know, the entire 100% unplanned mode.

00:33:37.940 --> 00:33:39.920
I won't say honest, I'll say unplanned

00:33:39.920 --> 00:33:41.230
'cause I do think there's a difference.

00:33:41.230 --> 00:33:45.030
- Right and I probably personally

00:33:47.790 --> 00:33:52.650
receive the brunt of that complaint more

00:33:52.650 --> 00:33:53.990
simply because...

00:33:56.920 --> 00:34:00.920
Okay, we did fact-based games on GMM.

00:34:00.920 --> 00:34:03.946
We did it back and forth
for a really long time,

00:34:03.946 --> 00:34:06.450
but those of you who've
sort of observed patterns

00:34:06.450 --> 00:34:08.913
of GMM probably learned that okay,

00:34:08.913 --> 00:34:12.370
if we're gonna play a fact-based game,

00:34:12.370 --> 00:34:13.760
I host it and you play it.

00:34:13.760 --> 00:34:16.160
That's just what we've arrived at.

00:34:16.160 --> 00:34:18.520
- Right.
- And because we each

00:34:18.520 --> 00:34:22.040
fit those roles better
than the other, right?

00:34:22.040 --> 00:34:25.220
It's like, you not having
any clue as to what

00:34:25.220 --> 00:34:27.320
I'm going to ask and what you're gonna say

00:34:28.170 --> 00:34:31.900
sets you up to say really funny things.

00:34:31.900 --> 00:34:33.343
Funnier things than I would be saying

00:34:33.343 --> 00:34:37.583
if I was just thinking of
things off the top of my head.

00:34:38.612 --> 00:34:42.030
But I'm pretty good at saying jokes

00:34:42.030 --> 00:34:44.400
that I thought about ahead of time.

00:34:44.400 --> 00:34:47.093
So I think that,

00:34:47.930 --> 00:34:50.234
I don't know, I mean it's,

00:34:50.234 --> 00:34:53.214
I don't sit around
thinking about this a lot,

00:34:53.214 --> 00:34:56.440
but there is this sort of underlying thing

00:34:56.440 --> 00:35:00.110
that I just kind of understand
that there is some percentage

00:35:00.110 --> 00:35:01.934
and I don't know what it is,

00:35:01.934 --> 00:35:03.668
of people who kinda perceive it,

00:35:03.668 --> 00:35:05.550
perceive the show in that way

00:35:05.550 --> 00:35:07.950
and then a lot of that
is kinda focused at me

00:35:07.950 --> 00:35:10.000
because I'm the one that's
the source of those things.

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:12.723
I might be the source of
more of the scripted jokes,

00:35:12.723 --> 00:35:16.990
I might be a source of
more of the negative,

00:35:16.990 --> 00:35:20.990
the negative energy when
it comes to the humor.

00:35:20.990 --> 00:35:23.820
And so people who are
like I don't like that,

00:35:23.820 --> 00:35:25.460
I don't like anything that's negative,

00:35:25.460 --> 00:35:27.090
I don't like anything that's scripted.

00:35:27.090 --> 00:35:28.410
It's just like okay well,

00:35:28.410 --> 00:35:30.420
I don't like what Rhett
brings to the show.

00:35:30.420 --> 00:35:32.040
So I think that when I
talk about those things,

00:35:32.040 --> 00:35:35.623
I end up kind of, that's where
the frustration comes from.

00:35:38.184 --> 00:35:42.640
